  "question": {
    "text": "The DC common-emitter current gain of an n-p-n transistor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn></math>. The potential difference applied across the collector and emitter of a transistor used in CE configuration is, <math><msub><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2&nbsp;</mn><mo>V</mo><mo>.</mo></math> If the collector resistance <math><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mo>k</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>, the base current <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> and the collector current <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> are"
  },
